### Replace tea.KeyMsg with tea.KeyPressMsg for Key Presses

Source: https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea/blob/v2.0.0/UPGRADE_GUIDE_V2.md

In Bubble Tea v2, `tea.KeyMsg` is now an interface. Use `tea.KeyPressMsg` to specifically handle key press events. For handling both presses and releases, use `tea.KeyMsg` and a type switch.

```go
// Before:
case tea.KeyMsg:
    switch msg.String() {
    case "q":
        return m, tea.Quit
    }

// After:
case tea.KeyPressMsg:
    switch msg.String() {
    case "q":
        return m, tea.Quit
    }

```

```go
case tea.KeyMsg:
    switch key := msg.(type) {
    case tea.KeyPressMsg:
        // key press
    case tea.KeyReleaseMsg:
        // key release
    }

```

--------------------------------

### Implement Update Method

Source: https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea/blob/v2.0.0/tutorials/basics/README.md

Handles incoming messages such as key presses to update the application state, including cursor movement and item selection.

```go
func (m model) Update(msg tea.Msg) (tea.Model, tea.Cmd) {
    switch msg := msg.(type) {
    case tea.KeyPressMsg:
        switch msg.String() {
        case "ctrl+c", "q":
            return m, tea.Quit
        case "up", "k":
            if m.cursor > 0 {
                m.cursor--
            }
        case "down", "j":
            if m.cursor < len(m.choices)-1 {
                m.cursor++
            }
        case "enter", "space":
            _, ok := m.selected[m.cursor]
            if ok {
                delete(m.selected, m.cursor)
            } else {
                m.selected[m.cursor] = struct{}{}
            }
        }
    }
    return m, nil
}
```

--------------------------------

### Update Model State in Bubble Tea (Go)

Source: https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea/blob/v2.0.0/README.md

The Update method handles incoming messages (like key presses) and modifies the application's model accordingly. It can also return a command to trigger further actions. This example demonstrates handling key presses to navigate and select items.

```go
func (m model) Update(msg tea.Msg) (tea.Model, tea.Cmd) {
    switch msg := msg.(type) {

    // Is it a key press?
    case tea.KeyPressMsg:

        // Cool, what was the actual key pressed?
        switch msg.String() {

        // These keys should exit the program.
        case "ctrl+c", "q":
            return m, tea.Quit

        // The "up" and "k" keys move the cursor up
        case "up", "k":
            if m.cursor > 0 {
                m.cursor--
            }

        // The "down" and "j" keys move the cursor down
        case "down", "j":
            if m.cursor < len(m.choices)-1 {
                m.cursor++
            }

        // The "enter" key and the space bar toggle the selected state
        // for the item that the cursor is pointing at.
        case "enter", "space":
            _, ok := m.selected[m.cursor]
            if ok {
                delete(m.selected, m.cursor)
            } else {
                m.selected[m.cursor] = struct{}{}
            }
        }
    }

    // Return the updated model to the Bubble Tea runtime for processing.
    // Note that we're not returning a command.
    return m, nil
}
```

--------------------------------

### Update Key Event Matching in Bubble Tea

Source: https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea/blob/v2.0.0/UPGRADE_GUIDE_V2.md

Demonstrates how to update key event handling from v1 to v2, including string-based matching and field-based matching for modifiers.

```go
// Before:
case tea.KeyCtrlC:
    // ctrl+c

// After (option A — string matching):
case tea.KeyPressMsg:
    switch msg.String() {
    case "ctrl+c":
        // ctrl+c
    }

// After (option B — field matching):
case tea.KeyPressMsg:
    if msg.Code == 'c' && msg.Mod == tea.ModCtrl {
        // ctrl+c
    }
```

### Bubble Tea v2.0.0 Release Notes > Ctrl+key matching

Source: https://github.com/charmbracelet/bubbletea/blob/v2.0.0/UPGRADE_GUIDE_V2.md

Matching Ctrl+key combinations has been updated. Previously, you might have used `case tea.KeyCtrlC:`. In v2, there are two recommended approaches. Option A involves string matching: `case tea.KeyPressMsg: switch msg.String() { case "ctrl+c": ... }`. Option B involves field matching: `case tea.KeyPressMsg: if msg.Code == 'c' && msg.Mod == tea.ModCtrl { ... }`. This provides more explicit control over how modifier keys are handled.

